# Environment variables this repository reads.
#
# The scripts under scripts/ read the process environment, so export these in your
# shell — or `set -a; . ./.env; set +a`. `wrangler dev` is the one thing that reads
# a `.env` file itself, for variables on `env` during local development — except
# when a `.dev.vars` exists, which displaces it entirely; secrets belong there (see
# `.dev.vars.example`). A real `.env` stays out of git, as `.gitignore` ensures.
#
# Not in this file, because they are not environment variables:
#   - GITHUB_USERNAME and SYSTEM_ENV are Worker vars in wrangler.toml [vars].
#   - The Worker's secrets (ATV_GDRIVE_MAP, ATV_TORBOX_API_KEY, ATV_DEVICE_TOKEN,
#     ATV_STREAM_SIGNING_KEY) are set with `wrangler secret put` for a deploy and
#     go in `.dev.vars` for `wrangler dev` — see `.dev.vars.example`. Not exported here.
#   - The repository secrets CI uses (CF_API_TOKEN, ATV_SEED_CONFIG, the Android
#     signing key) live in GitHub, not on a developer's machine.
# `docs/continuity.md` holds every name above and how to rebuild it.

# Where `pnpm smoke` and `pnpm streams` point when no URL is given on the command
# line. The sandbox is the default deploy target of this branch.
ATV_BASE_URL=

# Cloudflare Access service token. Both are needed together; when set, smoke and
# check-streams send them as CF-Access-Client-Id / -Client-Secret so they can
# reach a host behind a login. Unset is normal for the workers.dev sandbox.
CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID=
CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Two escape hatches, deliberately awkward to type. Leave them unset.
#
# Let seeding and deploying write production's KV ids (scripts/seed-kv.mjs and
# check-deploy-target.mjs refuse without it).
#ATV_ALLOW_SHARED_KV=
#
# Let `pnpm deploy:prod` replace atv-core-orchestrator. This branch must not.
#ATV_ALLOW_PROD_DEPLOY=

# Treat an unseeded lineup as a pass in `pnpm smoke`, for a freshly created
# sandbox namespace. Equivalent to the --allow-empty flag.
#ATV_SMOKE_ALLOW_EMPTY=
